## Steering (no need to interrupt)

- **Add work:** file an issue (the Feature / backlog item form) and label it `ready`. **Reorder:** `priority:high` / `priority:low`. **Pause one:** remove `ready`. On the `in-progress` issue that pauses the whole loop (`⏸ LOOP PAUSED` — it never starts another slice meanwhile); relabel it to resume, or remove `in-progress` to drop it. **Hold a big one:** split it into sub-issues — the parent is never worked, its sub-issues are. The loop re-reads the queue at every iteration.
- **Edit this runbook** while the loop runs — outside Claude (in the enforce modes the guard denies Claude writing it; its `ready-approvers` decide whose issues the loop trusts). With the guard enforcing, the loop commits your uncommitted `openspec/AUTOLOOP.md` edit to `main` at its next Phase 0. In `log` it can't tell your edit from a worker's, so it stops and asks you to commit it yourself.
- **Change the guard mode** (`.claude/hooks/merge-guard.mode`) outside Claude — in a terminal, or with `!` in front of the command at the Claude Code prompt. It applies to the next tool call; commit it yourself (the loop never commits it and reminds you in its report).
- **Emergency stop:** create the kill-switch file (`kill-switch:` above — by default `touch openspec/STOP`). The loop checks it before every phase and every merge, and halts leaving any open PRs as they are. Delete the file to resume.
- **Hard stop now:** interrupt the running `/loop` (Esc), or tell it to stop.

## Merge guard

`.claude/hooks/merge-guard.sh` runs as a `PreToolUse` hook on every Bash, Write/Edit and `mcp__*` call (it needs `python3`). It independently re-checks every merge (`gh pr merge` in any form, `gh api` merge endpoints, MCP merge tools) on the PR and repository the command really targets: all checks on the head commit green, and the newest `### 🤖` gate review **posted by the loop's GitHub account** reads PASS **on the current head commit**. In `enforce` it also denies subagent merges and pushes to `main`, any push to `main` beyond `openspec/**`, force-pushes and deletes of `main` however spelled, applying the `ready` label (from anyone), a subagent posting a `### 🤖` verdict, Claude — main conversation included — writing the guard's script, mode file, `.claude/settings*.json` or this runbook (also by `git restore`/`checkout -- <path>`, a patch, or a `git stash`/`reset --hard` that would discard your uncommitted edit of one), and any command mentioning a merge, push, guard file or `ready` that it cannot parse (run gh/git directly, with literal arguments). It fails closed when it can't reach GitHub. Mode lives in `.claude/hooks/merge-guard.mode` (`off | log | enforce-ci | enforce`; missing or garbled = `enforce-ci`), re-read on every invocation — no restart needed. Every decision is logged to `.claude/hooks/merge-guard.log`.

**Trust ramp.** It starts in `log` so it cannot block a real merge before you have seen it agree with the loop. After an increment or two, check the log, then switch the mode yourself — in a terminal, or with `!` in front of each command at the Claude Code prompt (once it enforces, Claude can't write the mode file):

```bash
cut -f2,5,6 .claude/hooks/merge-guard.log   # every merge the loop performed should show ALLOW
echo enforce > .claude/hooks/merge-guard.mode
git commit -m "chore(autoloop): enforce merge guard" -- .claude/hooks/merge-guard.mode && git push
```

The mode file is tracked — it's project policy, so a fresh clone keeps it. Only you commit it: the loop leaves an uncommitted mode change alone and reminds you in its report.

**Branch protection.** If `main` requires pull requests, the account the loop runs as needs a bypass for its `openspec/**` bookkeeping pushes — see [Branch protection](https://github.com/arthurnagy/kmp-forge/blob/main/docs/autoloop.md#branch-protection).

From then on "never force-merge" is enforced by the harness rather than trusted to the model.

## When it escalates

The loop prints a `⛔ ESCALATION` block and stops when it can't safely proceed: CI still red after 2 fix cycles, a gate BLOCK, a gate still not PASS in round 3 (after two fix cycles), a code-review ERROR (the diff couldn't be established), a worker returning FAILED, an issue's unmet **Needs a human first** precondition, a merge-guard denial, a PR a human closed, two `in-progress` issues at once, or uncommitted changes other than your steering edits. Read the block, make the one decision it asks for, then relaunch `/loop /kmp-forge-next-increment`.
